# =====================================================================================================================
# .env.example - EVERY setting this repo reads, what it is for, and where to find the value. It is AUTHORITATIVE: there is no other list.
#
# How the files work (the shared config, loaded in this order - later wins):
#     .env  ->  .env.<ENV>  ->  .env.<ENV>.local  ->  real environment variables (PowerShell: $env:NAME = "value")
#   * Pick the file with ENV (default qa): ENV=dev runs with .env.dev. In PowerShell: $env:ENV = "dev"
#   * .env.<ENV> (.env.qa, .env.dev, .env.stage, .env.simulator) is committed and holds NO secrets.
#   * .env.<ENV>.local (e.g. .env.qa.local) is git-ignored and holds the SECRETS. The template is .env.local.example: copy it to
#     .env.<ENV>.local. (A file named just .env.local is NOT read.)
#   * A variable set in your shell wins over every file. If a value "does not take", look at: Get-ChildItem Env:SAS_*
#
# Each variable has a comment block directly above it:
#     What      what it is for
#     Values    what is accepted
#     Default   what applies when it is not set
#     Required  whether you must set it, and when
#     Secret    NO, or YES = it belongs in .env.<ENV>.local (or a CI "masked" variable), never in a committed file
#     Find it   where to read the value in the SAS programs / on the machine
# The assignment line under each block is an example you can paste. Optional variables are commented out (a leading "# "): remove the
# "# " to use them. Do not leave one active with an empty value.
#
# Quoting: write a value with special characters (# $ spaces, a leading or trailing space) in double quotes: SAS_PASSWORD="my#pass word".
# An unquoted # starts a comment and silently cuts the value. Inside double quotes \n is a line break: use single quotes for a password
# that contains a backslash.
#
# tests/envDocs.spec.ts keeps this file, the README's "Configuration reference" table and .env.local.example in step with the code: a variable
# the code reads that is missing here, or a variable listed here that nothing reads, fails the test run.
#
# NOT VERIFIED: nothing in this file has been checked against a real SAS installation on a real Windows desktop (none was available).
# Where it says where to find a value in a SAS program, that is from SAS documentation and community sources, not from a machine.
# =====================================================================================================================


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. FIRST THINGS TO FILL IN - enough to run the first test against your SAS programs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# On the first run: copy .env.local.example to .env.<ENV>.local for the secret, fill in the lines below, run `npm run doctor`.

# What:     which .env.<ENV> file (and .env.<ENV>.local) is loaded; also the default of SAS_ENVIRONMENT.
# Values:   any name you have a file for: dev, qa, stage, simulator ...
# Default:  qa
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it. PowerShell: $env:ENV = "dev"   (undo: Remove-Item Env:ENV)
ENV=qa

# What:     the SAS environment this run works against. Every driver refuses an environment that is not declared in
#           SAS_ENVIRONMENTS_FILE and every host that is not on that environment's allow-list. With the stand-in desktop and no value here
#           the run is the declared `simulator` environment (it contacts nothing); with the stand-in this setting is ignored.
# Values:   a name declared in the environments file (letters, digits, "_" and "-", up to 32 characters; case is ignored)
# Default:  the value of ENV (with the stand-in, SAS_DESKTOP_SIMULATOR=true: always simulator)
# Required: no (the default is usually right); the NAME must be declared or every operation is refused
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  your team's environments file (SAS_ENVIRONMENTS_FILE): env/sas-environments.json
# SAS_ENVIRONMENT=dev

# What:     the full path of SAS Management Console's launcher, so the fixtures can START the console. Not needed to attach to a
#           console that is already running (SAS_APP_MODE=attach).
# Values:   a path to sasmc.exe
# Default:  (none)
# Required: YES to launch Management Console from a test (the *App / *LoggedOn fixtures)
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  Start menu > right-click "SAS Management Console 9.4" > Open file location > right-click the shortcut > Properties > Target.
#           Typically below C:\Program Files\SASHome\SASManagementConsole\9.4 (NOT VERIFIED: the folder differs between installations).
# SAS_SMC_PATH=C:\Program Files\SASHome\SASManagementConsole\9.4\sasmc.exe

# What:     the metadata profile Management Console connects to: the entry of the console's connection dialog, by name.
# Values:   the profile name exactly as the dialog lists it (case and spaces count)
# Default:  (none)
# Required: YES when the console asks for a profile at start
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  start the console by hand: the first dialog ("Connection Profile") lists the profiles. It is the name of the one you pick.
# SAS_SMC_PROFILE=Dev Metadata Server

# What:     the full path of SAS Enterprise Guide, so the fixtures can START it for the screen tests. Not needed for the COM route
#           (egCom) or to attach to a running Enterprise Guide.
# Values:   a path to SEGuide.exe
# Default:  (none)
# Required: YES to launch Enterprise Guide from a test (the eg / egApp / egLoggedOn fixtures)
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  Start menu > right-click "SAS Enterprise Guide" > Open file location > Properties > Target.
#           Typically below C:\Program Files\SASHome\SASEnterpriseGuide\<major version> (NOT VERIFIED).
# SAS_EG_PATH=C:\Program Files\SASHome\SASEnterpriseGuide\8\SEGuide.exe

# What:     the Enterprise Guide server profile to activate: the COM route calls Application.SetActiveProfile with it, the screen route
#           chooses it in the connect dialog.
# Values:   the profile name as Enterprise Guide lists it
# Default:  (none: the profile Enterprise Guide used last)
# Required: no (YES when projects must run on a specific server)
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  Enterprise Guide: the "Select Profile" dialog of Connect, or Tools > Connections: the name of the server entry.
# SAS_EG_PROFILE=Dev SAS Server

# What:     the user name of your personal SAS login, used for every program that has no SAS_<APP>_USER of its own.
# Values:   the user name as you type it at the SAS logon, e.g. ajoshi or DOMAIN\ajoshi
# Default:  (none)
# Required: YES whenever a *LoggedOn fixture logs on (unless every program has its own SAS_<APP>_USER)
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  the user name you use to log on to SAS Management Console / Enterprise Guide. A dedicated test account with least privilege is the target state.
# SAS_USER=ajoshi

# What:     the password of SAS_USER. A WRONG password counts towards the account lockout: see SAS_LOGON_FAILURE_COOLDOWN_MIN.
# Values:   the password. Special characters (# $ spaces) need double quotes: SAS_PASSWORD="my#pass word"
# Default:  (none)
# Required: YES whenever a *LoggedOn fixture logs on
# Secret:   YES - put it in .env.<ENV>.local (or a masked CI variable), never in a committed file
# Find it:  your password manager. Put it in .env.<ENV>.local (template: .env.local.example) or a masked CI variable, never in a committed file. It is never printed, logged or written to the audit log.
# SAS_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. ENVIRONMENTS AND SAFETY - what a run may contact and change (see docs/SECURITY.md)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# What:     the committed JSON file in which the team declares its SAS environments once: for each name its kind (nonprod or prod)
#           and the host patterns (metadata servers, web addresses, UNC paths) a driver may contact. No secrets in it, ever. The kind and hosts come ONLY
#           from this file, so a variable in someone's shell cannot turn production into "nonprod".
#           Shape: { "environments": { "dev": { "kind": "nonprod", "hosts": ["sasmeta.dev.example.com", "**.dev.example.com:8561"] } } }
#           Patterns: "*" = part of one name label (no dots), "**" = anything including dots; ":port" restricts the port.
#           The desktop programs reach SAS through their own saved profiles, so the host list cannot be enforced for their steps: the kind, the
#           namespace rule and the audit log are (docs/SECURITY.md).
# Values:   a path to the file
# Default:  env/sas-environments.json (it declares only `simulator`; env/sas-environments.json.example shows dev, qa, stage and prod)
# Required: YES for any run that does something (the default file location counts); if the file does not exist NO environment is known and every operation is refused
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  your SAS administrator knows the host names; the file is reviewed like code. Copy the entries you need from env/sas-environments.json.example.
# SAS_ENVIRONMENTS_FILE=env/sas-environments.json

# What:     allows READ operations against an environment of kind "prod". Production is read-only by construction: this setting never
#           allows a change there, and nothing else does either.
# Values:   true | false (also 1/0, yes/no, on/off)
# Default:  false
# Required: no; set it only for a run that must read production
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it; agree it with the owner of the production system first
# SAS_ALLOW_PROD_READS=false

# What:     lets a run change objects OUTSIDE its own test namespace on a nonprod environment (normally a test may only change objects
#           named with run.ns(...), i.e. AUTO_<runId>_...: that includes running an Enterprise Guide project or program that has its own name).
#           Needs SAS_ALLOW_OUTSIDE_NAMESPACE_REASON. A warning is logged and every audit record of the run is marked "override". It does not lift the
#           production rule, the host allow-list or the checks on the target.
# Values:   true | false
# Default:  false
# Required: no; for the deliberate run of an existing project, clean-up of leftovers or one-off set-up only
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it
# SAS_ALLOW_OUTSIDE_NAMESPACE=false

# What:     why the run may change objects outside its namespace. Written to the audit log with each such action.
# Values:   free text, at least 8 characters (a ticket number and a sentence)
# Default:  (none)
# Required: YES whenever SAS_ALLOW_OUTSIDE_NAMESPACE=true (the policy refuses otherwise)
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you write it. It goes to the audit log: put no secret in it.
# SAS_ALLOW_OUTSIDE_NAMESPACE_REASON=Run the nightly Sales project, ticket SAS-123

# What:     the name pattern of the test namespace: objects a run creates are named with it, and only objects named with it may be changed.
# Values:   text containing {runId} exactly once, starting with at least 3 literal characters; letters, digits, space, "_", "." and "-"
# Default:  AUTO_{runId}
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it; agree it with the SAS administrators so nobody creates real objects with that prefix
# SAS_NAMESPACE_PATTERN=AUTO_{runId}

# What:     the id of the run; names the test namespace (AUTO_<id>_...), the audit file and the evidence folder. playwright.config.ts calls
#           initRunContext() so every worker of one run shares one id.
# Values:   3 to 24 letters and digits (no "_" or "-")
# Default:  generated: a UTC time stamp plus 4 random hex digits, e.g. 261023214501a3f9
# Required: no (a CI pipeline can pass its number to make runs traceable)
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  CI: the pipeline or job number without punctuation
# SAS_RUN_ID=ci4711

# What:     run the pre-flight health gate once per worker before the first test: the environment is declared, the state folder and audit log
#           are writable, no logon cooldown is active, the bridge answers, the desktop is interactive and unlocked. A red gate fails ONCE with every red
#           check and what to do, instead of hundreds of timeouts.
# Values:   true | false
# Default:  true
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it. Switch it off only to see a single test fail on its own.
# SAS_PREFLIGHT=true

# What:     the default time limit of one pre-flight check before it counts as red (so a dead server fails once, quickly).
# Values:   milliseconds, a whole number
# Default:  10000
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it
# SAS_PREFLIGHT_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. CREDENTIALS AND LOGON PROTECTION
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One personal login for both programs (SAS_USER / SAS_PASSWORD) or one per program (SAS_SMC_USER / SAS_SMC_PASSWORD, SAS_EG_USER /
# SAS_EG_PASSWORD). User and password are always taken as a PAIR from one source: a half-set pair is an error, never a mixed logon.

# What:     a login for ONE program, replacing SAS_USER for it. <APP> is the program in capitals: SMC (SAS Management Console) or EG (SAS Enterprise
#           Guide). The user and the password of a program are taken together: set both or neither.
# Values:   as SAS_USER
# Default:  (none: SAS_USER is used)
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  as SAS_USER
# SAS_<APP>_USER=ajoshi

# What:     the password for SAS_<APP>_USER (SAS_SMC_PASSWORD, SAS_EG_PASSWORD).
# Values:   the password; double quotes for special characters
# Default:  (none: SAS_PASSWORD is used)
# Required: YES when SAS_<APP>_USER is set
# Secret:   YES - put it in .env.<ENV>.local (or a masked CI variable), never in a committed file
# Find it:  your password manager. Put it in .env.<ENV>.local or a masked CI variable, never in a committed file.
# SAS_<APP>_PASSWORD=

# What:     minutes during which the same environment + program + user + password is NOT typed again after the program rejected it. A rejected
#           logon is never retried: repeated wrong logons lock the account. A changed password or user clears the stop by itself.
# Values:   0 (protection off - not advised on a shared system) or a number of minutes
# Default:  15
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it; the lockout limit itself is your organisation's security policy. The marker is .sas-state/logon-failed.json (SAS_STATE_DIR): delete it once the password is fixed.
# SAS_LOGON_FAILURE_COOLDOWN_MIN=15

# What:     the folder for state that must outlive a run: the logon-failure marker (logon-failed.json) and the list of created objects
#           (created-objects.jsonl). Deliberately not under test-results, which Playwright empties at the start of every run.
# Values:   a folder path (git-ignored by .gitignore: .sas-state/)
# Default:  .sas-state
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it. In CI point it at a folder that survives between pipelines (a cache) if re-runs should be stopped too. The marker holds a salted fingerprint of a rejected password, never the password; the files are owner-only.
# SAS_STATE_DIR=.sas-state

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. SAS MANAGEMENT CONSOLE (Java Swing; its screens are the LAST resort)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SAS_SMC_PATH and SAS_SMC_PROFILE are in section 1. Management Console needs the Java Access Bridge (`jabswitch -enable`, then restart the
# console): without it its window is empty to Windows UI Automation. `npm run doctor` checks it. NOT VERIFIED on a real console.

# What:     how the app fixtures get their program (both Management Console and Enterprise Guide's screens).
# Values:   launch = start the program from SAS_SMC_PATH / SAS_EG_PATH and close it at the end of the test
#           attach = use the program that is already open, never start or close it (start it logged on or at its logon dialog yourself)
#           auto   = launch when the program's path is set, else attach
#           (with the stand-in desktop the programs are always launched)
# Default:  auto
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it. Attach is the safe choice on a desktop where someone has the console open.
# SAS_APP_MODE=auto

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. SAS ENTERPRISE GUIDE (the COM route first, its screens second)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SAS_EG_PATH and SAS_EG_PROFILE are in section 1. The COM route (the egCom fixture) needs no window and no unlocked desktop beyond starting
# Enterprise Guide in your session; the screens (eg, egApp, egLoggedOn) need the interactive desktop. NOT VERIFIED on a real Enterprise Guide.

# What:     the exact COM ProgID of Enterprise Guide's automation object model. It carries the version number.
# Values:   SASEGObjectModel.Application.<version>
# Default:  (none: discovered - the newest registered SASEGObjectModel.Application.<version>, else the known versions newest first)
# Required: no (set it when discovery picks the wrong version or cannot find yours)
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  Enterprise Guide: Help > About shows the version (e.g. 8.3). `npm run doctor` and `npm run inspect` print what is registered; PowerShell:
#           Get-ChildItem Registry::HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT | Where-Object PSChildName -like 'SASEGObjectModel.Application.*'
# SAS_EG_PROGID=SASEGObjectModel.Application.8.3

# What:     the COM class allow-list handed to the bridge when it starts: the ONLY ProgIDs com.create will create. Everything else is refused by the
#           bridge itself (and cannot be widened by a command).
# Values:   comma-separated ProgIDs in which * is the only wildcard (letters, digits, _ and . only)
# Default:  SASEGObjectModel.Application.*,SASObjectManager.*
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  add a class only after a security review (docs/SECURITY.md)
# SAS_COM_PROGIDS=SASEGObjectModel.Application.*,SASObjectManager.*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. COMMAND-LINE TOOLS (the process driver: the FIRST route, no window involved)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by src/process. NOT VERIFIED against any real SAS tool: no option name, exit code or output format is built in.

# What:     the folders command-line tools may be run from. A tool name is looked up ONLY here (never on PATH or in the working directory) and a link
#           that leads out of a folder is refused. An absolute path outside them is accepted unless the driver is built with allowExplicitPaths: false.
# Values:   comma-separated folder paths (a comma cannot be part of a path here)
# Default:  (none: only explicit absolute paths can be run)
# Required: YES to run a tool by name
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  the install folder of the SAS client software, e.g. the SAS Platform Object Framework folder (run scripts/inventory-sas.ps1; NOT VERIFIED)
# SAS_TOOL_DIRS=C:\Program Files\SASHome\SASPlatformObjectFramework\9.4

# What:     the variables of the parent environment a command-line tool inherits. Everything else is removed from the child's environment. A name
#           that looks like a secret (password, token, key ...) is refused here: pass secrets as secretArgs, on standard input or as an explicit entry of the call.
# Values:   comma-separated variable names
# Default:  PATH,SystemRoot,windir,TEMP,TMP,TMPDIR,HOME,USERPROFILE,APPDATA,LOCALAPPDATA,ProgramData,ProgramFiles,ProgramFiles(x86),COMPUTERNAME,USERNAME,USERDOMAIN,LANG,LC_ALL,TZ,JAVA_HOME
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  the tool's documentation says what it needs
# SAS_CHILD_ENV_ALLOW=PATH,SystemRoot,TEMP,TMP,JAVA_HOME

# What:     the default time limit of one command-line tool run. When it is exceeded the program AND everything it started are ended.
# Values:   milliseconds, 1 or more
# Default:  120000
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it
# SAS_PROCESS_TIMEOUT_MS=120000

# What:     the most output kept per stream (stdout and stderr each); more is dropped, the run is not stopped, and the result says "truncated".
# Values:   bytes, 1 or more
# Default:  1048576
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it
# SAS_PROCESS_MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES=1048576

# What:     the JSON file with the option tables of the command-line tools (flags, exit-code meanings, output parser) for the BatchToolRunner.
#           Nothing about any SAS tool is built in: copy the options from the documentation of the INSTALLED release. Every table is NOT VERIFIED until a
#           conformance run on a machine with the real tool has passed ("verified": true).
#           Shape: { "tools": { "<name>": { "command": "...", "kind": "read", "options": { "<logical>": { "flag": "-x" } }, "exitCodes": { "0": { "meaning": "success", "ok": true } }, "parse": "lines" } } }
# Values:   a path to the file
# Default:  (none: no tables)
# Required: only to use the BatchToolRunner with a file
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  the tool documentation of your SAS release
# SAS_BATCH_TOOLS_FILE=env/sas-batch-tools.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. TRANSPORT - how the framework reaches the Windows desktop
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- PowerShell (this machine) ---

# What:     how the framework reaches the Windows desktop: the PowerShell bridge on THIS machine, or on a remote desktop host.
# Values:   powershell = start bridge/sas-desktop-bridge.ps1 here (a Windows machine with an unlocked desktop)
#           remote     = run the same script on the remote desktop host through the RDP agent (needs REMOTE_ENABLED=true, the REMOTE_* keys below, and
#                        REMOTE_ALLOW including "sap": the capability that lets the agent host helper processes)
# Default:  powershell
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it. A remote host (an AWS WorkSpace reached only by RDP) means exactly one worker.
# SAS_DESKTOP_TRANSPORT=powershell

# What:     the PowerShell that runs the bridge. An older (32-bit) Enterprise Guide registers its COM classes for 32-bit programs only: then the
#           32-bit PowerShell is needed or the class is "not registered".
# Values:   a path or a program name
# Default:  powershell.exe (Windows PowerShell 5.1, 64-bit on a 64-bit Windows)
# Required: no (YES for a 32-bit Enterprise Guide)
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  the 32-bit one is C:\Windows\SysWOW64\WindowsPowerShell\v1.0\powershell.exe. `npm run doctor` says when the COM class is "not registered".
# SAS_DESKTOP_POWERSHELL=C:\Windows\SysWOW64\WindowsPowerShell\v1.0\powershell.exe

# What:     a folder holding an approved - typically code-signed - copy of the bridge script, named sas-desktop-bridge.ps1. That file is run in place
#           WITHOUT -ExecutionPolicy Bypass, so the machine's own policy (AllSigned ...) decides. The copy must be the exact script this version ships (a
#           signature block is ignored): an edited or stale copy is refused (SHA-256 check).
# Values:   a folder path
# Default:  (none: the shipped script is copied to a private temporary file and run with -ExecutionPolicy Bypass for that one process)
# Required: no (YES on a desktop where unsigned scripts must not run)
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  your security team signs the script (`npm run bridge:path` prints its path and expected SHA-256)
# SAS_DESKTOP_SCRIPT_DIR=C:\ProgramData\sas-automation\approved

# --- Remote desktop host (SAS_DESKTOP_TRANSPORT=remote; the REMOTE_* keys are read by referenced-automation-utils, see its docs/REMOTE_DESKTOP.md) ---

# What:     with SAS_DESKTOP_TRANSPORT=remote: an approved bridge script that already lives ON THE REMOTE HOST; run by path instead of sending the
#           shipped script text through the agent (its hash then cannot be checked from here: control it on the host).
# Values:   a path on the remote host
# Default:  (none: the shipped, hash-verified script text is sent through the agent)
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  your security team
# SAS_DESKTOP_REMOTE_BRIDGE_FILE=C:\ProgramData\sas-automation\approved\sas-desktop-bridge.ps1

# What:     turns the remote desktop client of referenced-automation-utils on (read by that package, not by this repo).
# Values:   true | false
# Default:  false
# Required: YES with SAS_DESKTOP_TRANSPORT=remote
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it; setup, the other REMOTE_* keys and troubleshooting: referenced-automation-utils docs/REMOTE_DESKTOP.md
# REMOTE_ENABLED=true

# What:     the remote desktop host (read by the utils package).
# Values:   a host name or address, no scheme, path or spaces
# Default:  (none)
# Required: YES with REMOTE_ENABLED=true
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  the address you use in Remote Desktop Connection
# REMOTE_RDP_HOST=workspace.example.com

# What:     the RDP port of the host.
# Values:   1 to 65535
# Default:  3389
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  your network team
# REMOTE_RDP_PORT=3389

# What:     the Windows login on the remote host, with the domain as DOMAIN\user or in REMOTE_RDP_DOMAIN.
# Values:   a user name
# Default:  (none)
# Required: YES with REMOTE_AGENT_START=alternate-shell
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  the login you use in Remote Desktop Connection
# REMOTE_RDP_USER=DOMAIN\ajoshi

# What:     the Windows password on the remote host. It is not a SAS password.
# Values:   the password
# Default:  (none)
# Required: YES with REMOTE_AGENT_START=alternate-shell
# Secret:   YES - put it in .env.<ENV>.local (or a masked CI variable), never in a committed file
# Find it:  your password manager. Put it in .env.<ENV>.local or a masked CI variable, never in a committed file.
# REMOTE_RDP_PASSWORD=

# What:     the Windows domain of REMOTE_RDP_USER (when it is not written DOMAIN\user).
# Values:   a domain name
# Default:  (none)
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  your IT team
# REMOTE_RDP_DOMAIN=CORP

# What:     the folder on this machine that appears as \\tsclient\<drive> inside the RDP session: the agent's mailbox.
# Values:   a folder path
# Default:  .remote-mailbox
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it (git-ignored)
# REMOTE_SHARE_DIR=.remote-mailbox

# What:     how the agent gets started inside the RDP session of the host.
# Values:   alternate-shell = this machine logs in and starts the agent instead of Explorer
#           startup = the agent was installed once in the host's Startup folder
#           manual = you start it yourself
# Default:  alternate-shell
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it
# REMOTE_AGENT_START=alternate-shell

# What:     the capabilities the remote agent may be asked for. The desktop bridge needs "sap" (the capability that lets the agent host helper processes;
#           it is the same one the SAP bridge uses).
# Values:   comma-separated: http, db, sap, exec (exec runs arbitrary commands and is never on by default)
# Default:  http,db,sap
# Required: no (YES to include sap if you narrowed it)
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it; leave exec off
# REMOTE_ALLOW=http,db,sap

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. EVIDENCE AND TIMING
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A screenshot of an administration screen can show personal data: evidence is only taken for a failed test unless you ask for more, the folder
# is the run's own and other runs' folders are pruned, and the bridge paints password fields black before it takes one.

# What:     when the fixtures keep a screenshot (and, for a failure, the step journal) in the report. The master switch.
# Values:   off     = nothing automatic (evidence a test asks for with app.evidence(...) is always taken)
#           failure = only for a test that failed
#           on      = after every test, pass or fail
#           steps   = failure, plus a screenshot after every step that may change something (slow; for diagnosing a flow)
# Default:  failure
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it; agree it with your data-protection rules
# SAS_EVIDENCE=failure

# What:     the folder under which each run's evidence goes (<folder>/<runId>/). A file name can never climb out of it.
# Values:   a folder path
# Default:  test-results/sas-evidence
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it; CI: collect it as a job artifact
# SAS_EVIDENCE_DIR=test-results/sas-evidence

# What:     how long another run's evidence folder is kept before it is removed when a new run starts (by last change).
# Values:   a number of days; 0 keeps everything
# Default:  7
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  agree it with your data-protection rules
# SAS_EVIDENCE_RETENTION_DAYS=7

# What:     the desktop layer's own evidence switch. The fixtures follow SAS_EVIDENCE; this one only adds per-step screenshots: `steps` does what
#           SAS_EVIDENCE=steps does. SAS_EVIDENCE=off wins over it.
# Values:   off | failure | steps
# Default:  off
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it
# SAS_DESKTOP_EVIDENCE=off

# What:     where a desktop session writes evidence when it is NOT opened by the fixtures (the sas-desktop-inspect tool, your own scripts). The fixtures
#           always write under SAS_EVIDENCE_DIR/<runId>, so that retention applies.
# Values:   a folder path, relative to the folder you run from
# Default:  test-results/sas-desktop-evidence
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it
# SAS_DESKTOP_EVIDENCE_DIR=test-results/sas-desktop-evidence

# What:     how long one UI step waits for its control to exist, to be enabled and for the program to go idle. The framework polls; it never sleeps.
# Values:   milliseconds, a positive whole number
# Default:  30000
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  raise it only after looking at the program: a logon or a project open can be slow
# SAS_DESKTOP_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# What:     the "bridge is hung" backstop for ONE request to the bridge (not a screen wait). A request that gets no answer stops the bridge.
# Values:   milliseconds, a positive whole number
# Default:  60000
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it
# SAS_DESKTOP_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=60000

# What:     how long an assertion step ("Then the window title contains ...", "Then the control named ... is shown") keeps re-checking before it fails.
# Values:   milliseconds, a whole number
# Default:  5000
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  raise it for a slow program
# SAS_ASSERT_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# What:     how many Playwright workers run tests at once (read by the shared Playwright config; this repo reads it only to explain why it is ignored).
#           A REAL desktop is one desktop: with Windows or SAS_DESKTOP_TRANSPORT=remote (and no simulator) the run gets exactly one worker whatever this says, and the
#           desktopHost fixture refuses --workers=N. With SAS_DESKTOP_SIMULATOR=true every worker has its own stand-in and this is honoured.
# Values:   a number, or a percentage of the cores (50%)
# Default:  (Playwright's default)
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it
# WORKERS=4

# What:     how often Playwright repeats a failed test (read by the shared Playwright config). A rejected logon is never repeated by a retry: the logon guard refuses it.
# Values:   a whole number
# Default:  0 (1 on CI)
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it
# RETRIES=0

# What:     the time limit of one test (read by the shared Playwright config).
# Values:   milliseconds
# Default:  60000
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  raise it for a long project run
# TEST_TIMEOUT_MS=60000

# What:     the default time of Playwright's own web-first assertions (read by the shared Playwright config; the desktop assertions use SAS_ASSERT_TIMEOUT_MS / their own 5 s).
# Values:   milliseconds
# Default:  10000
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it
# EXPECT_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# What:     how much the shared logger writes (read by referenced-automation-utils from the real environment only).
# Values:   error | warn | info | debug
# Default:  info
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# What:     write the log to logs/automation.log as well as the console (read by referenced-automation-utils).
# Values:   true | false
# Default:  true
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it
# LOG_TO_FILE=true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. TEST DATA AND CLEANUP
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything a test creates in SAS is named inside the run's namespace (run.ns('name') = AUTO_<runId>_name) and registered with the cleanup fixture at
# the moment it is created: the cleanup runs after the test, pass or fail, newest first, while the program is still open.

# What:     what a clean-up that could not remove something does to its test.
# Values:   fail = the test FAILS with the list of what is still in SAS (it is also in the orphan record, .sas-state/created-objects.jsonl)
#           warn = only a test annotation and a log line
# Default:  fail
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it. A leftover object in a shared SAS system is a defect, not a log line: keep `fail`.
# SAS_CLEANUP_FAILURE=fail

# What:     the longest one clean-up (undo of a created object) may run before it is asked to stop (abort signal).
# Values:   milliseconds, 1 or more
# Default:  60000
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it
# SAS_CLEANUP_TIMEOUT_MS=60000

# What:     how long a clean-up that was asked to stop gets to actually finish; if it does not, the remaining clean-ups are not started (two clean-ups
#           working in one desktop session would corrupt each other) and are reported as not attempted.
# Values:   milliseconds, 0 or more
# Default:  10000
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it
# SAS_CLEANUP_GRACE_MS=10000

# What:     the audit log: one JSON line per privileged action (time, run id, correlation id, driver, environment, operation, kind, target, outcome, duration;
#           no secrets). Without it the default is one file per run, the newest SAS_AUDIT_KEEP_RUNS kept. Playwright empties test-results at the start of a run: point
#           this outside it to keep records across runs.
# Values:   a file path
# Default:  test-results/sas-audit/audit-<runId>.jsonl
# Required: no (but no privileged action runs when the audit log cannot be written)
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  CI: collect the folder as a job artifact for the security review
# SAS_AUDIT_FILE=test-results/sas-audit/audit.jsonl

# What:     how many runs' audit files are kept in the default folder (older ones are removed when a run starts writing). Not used with SAS_AUDIT_FILE.
# Values:   a whole number, 1 or more
# Default:  20
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it
# SAS_AUDIT_KEEP_RUNS=20

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 10. RECORDER - record what a person does in the programs and convert it to a test (ADR 0002; NOT VERIFIED on a real desktop)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `sas-desktop-record` records what a person does, `sas-desktop-to-playwright` turns the recording into a test, `sas-eg-import-script` turns Enterprise
# Guide's schedule script into a test (docs/RECORD_AND_PLAY.md). A recording NEVER contains a password, PIN, one-time code, token or key: the recorder keeps
# only the fact that something was typed into such a field, and the converted test reads the value from the environment - the variables at the end of
# this section (and SAS_USER, SAS_PASSWORD, SAS_SMC_PROFILE, SAS_EG_PROFILE, SAS_EG_PROGID, documented once, in sections 1 and 5). The recorder only starts
# against a declared environment, and production needs SAS_ALLOW_PROD_READS=true, exactly as for any other read.

# What:     the folder recordings are written to when `sas-desktop-record` is not given --out.
# Values:   a folder path (created when needed)
# Default:  recordings
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it
# SAS_RECORDER_DIR=recordings

# What:     the key chord that ends a recording. It is noticed in ANY window (so you can press it while another program has the focus) and is recorded
#           in none. Ctrl+C in the terminal ends it too.
# Values:   modifiers Ctrl, Alt, Shift joined with + and one key (a letter, a digit, F1..F24, Enter, Escape)
# Default:  Ctrl+Alt+Q
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  pick one the programs do not use
# SAS_RECORDER_STOP_KEY=Ctrl+Alt+Q

# What:     how the recorder listens. `full` = Windows mouse and keyboard hooks plus UI Automation events. `uia` = UI Automation events only (no hooks: for
#           a machine where hooks are not allowed; clicks on controls that raise no event, and keyboard shortcuts, are then not recorded).
# Values:   full, uia
# Default:  full
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  use uia where hooks are not allowed
# SAS_RECORDER_MODE=full

# What:     how often (milliseconds) the recorder collects the events the bridge has queued, and so how often the live "events seen" counter moves.
# Values:   a whole number from 20 to 5000
# Default:  200
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it
# SAS_RECORDER_POLL_MS=200

# What:     how many events the bridge keeps between two collections. When more arrive they are dropped and counted ("event(s) were lost" in the
#           summary) - raise this or lower SAS_RECORDER_POLL_MS if that happens.
# Values:   a whole number from 100 to 200000
# Default:  5000
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it
# SAS_RECORDER_MAX_QUEUE=5000

# What:     more fields to treat as secret, on top of the built-in rule (a password field, or a name or automation id that contains the word password,
#           pwd, pin, otp, token, secret or key). Use it for a field the recorder does not recognise - a Swing field whose accessible name does not say "password".
#           What is typed there is never recorded. Also available as --secret-pattern.
# Values:   comma-separated regular expressions, case-insensitive, matched against the control's name and automation id
# Default:  (none)
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose them
# SAS_RECORDER_SECRET_PATTERNS=account code,passcode

# What:     a PIN typed into a field named like one ("PIN", "userPin"): the converted test reads it from here. Read by the GENERATED test, not by this package.
# Values:   the PIN
# Default:  (none: the converted test fails with the name of the missing variable)
# Required: only when a recording typed into a PIN field
# Secret:   YES - put it in .env.<ENV>.local (or a masked CI variable), never in a committed file
# Find it:  put it in .env.<ENV>.local or a masked CI variable
# SAS_PIN=

# What:     a one-time code typed into a field named like one ("OTP"): the converted test reads it from here. A one-time code is only valid once and for a
#           short time: such a test needs a way to supply a fresh one for each run.
# Values:   the code
# Default:  (none: the converted test fails with the name of the missing variable)
# Required: only when a recording typed into an OTP field
# Secret:   YES - put it in .env.<ENV>.local (or a masked CI variable), never in a committed file
# Find it:  put it in .env.<ENV>.local or a masked CI variable
# SAS_OTP=

# What:     a token typed into a field named like one ("Access Token"): the converted test reads it from here.
# Values:   the token
# Default:  (none: the converted test fails with the name of the missing variable)
# Required: only when a recording typed into a token field
# Secret:   YES - put it in .env.<ENV>.local (or a masked CI variable), never in a committed file
# Find it:  put it in .env.<ENV>.local or a masked CI variable
# SAS_TOKEN=

# What:     a secret typed into a field named like one ("Client Secret", or a field matched by SAS_RECORDER_SECRET_PATTERNS): the converted test reads it from here.
# Values:   the secret
# Default:  (none: the converted test fails with the name of the missing variable)
# Required: only when a recording typed into such a field
# Secret:   YES - put it in .env.<ENV>.local (or a masked CI variable), never in a committed file
# Find it:  put it in .env.<ENV>.local or a masked CI variable
# SAS_SECRET=

# What:     a key typed into a field named like one ("API Key", "Encryption key"): the converted test reads it from here.
# Values:   the key
# Default:  (none: the converted test fails with the name of the missing variable)
# Required: only when a recording typed into a key field
# Secret:   YES - put it in .env.<ENV>.local (or a masked CI variable), never in a committed file
# Find it:  put it in .env.<ENV>.local or a masked CI variable
# SAS_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 11. SIMULATOR AND CI - run on any machine, and on the pipeline
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# What:     use the built-in stand-in desktop (an in-memory fake with a fake Management Console and a fake Enterprise Guide, and a fake Enterprise Guide
#           object model) instead of Windows. For trying the framework, the examples, the sample features and your own tests on any OS. It proves nothing about the real
#           programs. The stand-in is declared as the `simulator` environment and brings its own programs, connection profiles and demo login, so nothing else
#           needs to be configured. While it is in use SAS_ENVIRONMENT, SAS_SMC_PATH, SAS_SMC_PROFILE, SAS_EG_PATH, SAS_EG_PROFILE, SAS_EG_PROGID and the logins
#           (SAS_USER / SAS_PASSWORD, SAS_<APP>_USER / SAS_<APP>_PASSWORD) are IGNORED: a real password is never typed into a fake, and the tests behave the same
#           on every machine whatever .env.<ENV>.local holds. (A test can supply them on purpose through the `sasSettings` option.) For the duration of each test the
#           fixtures put the stand-in's own login and profile names into SAS_USER, SAS_PASSWORD, SAS_SMC_PROFILE and SAS_EG_PROFILE, so a converted recording that
#           reads them from the environment works unchanged; the environment is put back afterwards.
# Values:   true | false
# Default:  false
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it. `npm run try:simulator` sets it. ENV=simulator loads .env.simulator, which sets it.
# SAS_DESKTOP_SIMULATOR=true

# What:     run the tests of the real programs (tests/real and the scenarios of features/real) - the ones that start the REAL bridge and log on to the REAL Management Console and Enterprise Guide. Without
#           this (and without the stand-in desktop) they are skipped with the reason, so a plain `npm test` on a Windows machine that has SAS never opens the
#           programs or types a login. `npm run test:conformance`, `npm run test:real-samples` and `npm run test:real-smoke` set it for their own run (scripts/run-real.js), the same on
#           Windows, macOS and Linux; you do not set it by hand.
# Values:   true | false
# Default:  false
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you do not need to set it: use the two npm scripts above.
# SAS_REAL_TESTS=false

# What:     for the tests of this repo only: start the fake command-line tool as `node <script>` by its full path instead of by its own name. Windows runs only
#           .exe files, so the tests set this route themselves there; setting it on macOS or Linux takes the same route, which is how that route is checked
#           without a Windows machine. It changes no behaviour of the package.
# Values:   1 | (unset)
# Default:  (unset; on Windows the tests take this route by themselves)
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  you choose it: `SAS_TEST_NODE_LAUNCH=1 npm test` on macOS or Linux.
# SAS_TEST_NODE_LAUNCH=1

# What:     the least number of tests that must really run (passed or flaky): scripts/min-executed.js fails a run that discovers tests but skips them all - no Windows, no desktop -
#           and prints why the rest did not run.
# Values:   a whole number
# Default:  1
# Required: no (CI sets it through --min)
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  CI: the number of tests the job is expected to run
# MIN_EXECUTED=1

# What:     the npm registry the setup scripts install from (read by scripts/setup.sh and setup.bat).
# Values:   a URL
# Default:  (npm's configured registry)
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  your platform team; the same value as the CI/CD variable of the same name
# NPM_REGISTRY_URL=https://artifactory.example.com/api/npm/npm-virtual/

# What:     install the sibling @automation packages from ../shared-packages instead of the registry (read by the setup scripts).
# Values:   1 | (unset)
# Default:  (unset)
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  `.\scripts\setup.bat --local` does the same
# USE_LOCAL_PACKAGES=1

# What:     stand-in bridge fault injection (TEST EQUIPMENT ONLY - the real PowerShell bridge has no such switches): print a non-JSON line before the ready message.
# Values:   true | false
# Default:  false
# Required: no (tests only)
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  tests/desktop/transport.spec.ts
# BRIDGE_JUNK=false

# What:     stand-in bridge fault injection: never say ready.
# Values:   true | false
# Default:  false
# Required: no (tests only)
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  tests/desktop/transport.spec.ts
# BRIDGE_NO_READY=false

# What:     stand-in bridge fault injection: answer these operations late, so replies arrive out of order.
# Values:   comma-separated op:milliseconds
# Default:  (none)
# Required: no (tests only)
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  tests/desktop/transport.spec.ts
# BRIDGE_DELAY_OPS=ping:300

# What:     stand-in bridge fault injection: never answer these operations (a hung bridge).
# Values:   comma-separated operation names
# Default:  (none)
# Required: no (tests only)
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  tests/desktop/transport.spec.ts
# BRIDGE_HANG_OPS=windows

# What:     stand-in bridge fault injection: write to stderr and exit(3) when this operation arrives (a crashed bridge).
# Values:   an operation name
# Default:  (none)
# Required: no (tests only)
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  tests/desktop/transport.spec.ts
# BRIDGE_CRASH_OP=windows

# What:     stand-in bridge fault injection: close the read end of stdin after saying ready (the next write is EPIPE).
# Values:   true | false
# Default:  false
# Required: no (tests only)
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  tests/desktop/transport.spec.ts
# BRIDGE_CLOSE_STDIN=false

# What:     stand-in bridge fault injection: answer this operation with the code SESSION_LOST (a locked desktop).
# Values:   an operation name
# Default:  (none)
# Required: no (tests only)
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  tests/desktop/transport.spec.ts
# BRIDGE_SESSION_LOST_OP=windows


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 12. SAMPLE TESTS FOR A MACHINE WITH SAS - features/real/sample_*.feature (read-only; run with `npm run test:real-samples`)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The three samples only READ: nothing is created, changed or run on SAS. They need the settings of sections 1-7 (environment, login, program
# paths) and these, which say WHAT to look at on YOUR installation. NOT VERIFIED: none of them has been run against a real SAS installation.

# What:     the command-line sample (SAMPLE 1) runs this program and checks the exit code and the output. Pick a command that only prints something:
#           the version or help option of a tool in SAS_TOOL_DIRS (which options YOUR release has is in its documentation; NOT VERIFIED here).
#           Without it the test that runs a tool is skipped with the reason; the other two command-line samples need no tool.
# Values:   a tool name inside SAS_TOOL_DIRS, or an absolute path
# Default:  (none: that one test is skipped)
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  the tool folder of the SAS client software (run scripts/inventory-sas.ps1; NOT VERIFIED)
# SAS_SAMPLE_TOOL=C:\Program Files\SASHome\SASPlatformObjectFramework\9.4\ExportPackage.exe

# What:     the arguments of SAS_SAMPLE_TOOL, separated by commas (so an argument cannot contain a comma). Each is passed as it is: there is no shell.
# Values:   comma-separated text
# Default:  (no arguments)
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  the documentation of the tool
# SAS_SAMPLE_TOOL_ARGS=-help

# What:     a regular expression (case-insensitive) that the output of SAS_SAMPLE_TOOL must match: the proof that it is the program you meant.
# Values:   a regular expression
# Default:  (not checked)
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  run the command once by hand and copy a word it prints
# SAS_SAMPLE_TOOL_EXPECT=usage

# What:     the exit code of SAS_SAMPLE_TOOL that means success. Some tools print their help and exit with a non-zero code.
# Values:   a whole number
# Default:  0
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  run the command once by hand and look at the exit code (PowerShell: $LASTEXITCODE)
# SAS_SAMPLE_TOOL_EXIT=0

# What:     a user that the User Manager of Management Console must list in SAMPLE 2, spelled as the User Manager lists it (usually the display name,
#           which can differ from the login). Without it the sample only checks that the list is not empty.
# Values:   text
# Default:  (the list must be non-empty; the stand-in has "sasdemo")
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  Management Console, User Manager: the name in the left column
# SAS_SAMPLE_SMC_USER=Your Display Name

# What:     the full path of an existing Enterprise Guide project that SAMPLE 3 opens (opening only reads; the sample lists its process flows and
#           programs, then closes it without saving). Without it that test is skipped with the reason.
# Values:   a path on the machine where Enterprise Guide runs
# Default:  (none: that one test is skipped; the stand-in has its own)
# Required: no
# Secret:   NO
# Find it:  Enterprise Guide, File > Open > Project: pick a small project you own and copy its path
# SAS_SAMPLE_EG_PROJECT=C:\Projects\Sales.egp
